{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution has no 'agent' choosing to act; Popper's scientist must consciously choose a theory for application.","Survival in nature implies fitness for reproduction; survival of a theory in testing does not logically imply fitness for future prediction.","Evolutionary selection is blind; scientific preference is argued by Popper to be rational."],"overturn_conditions":["If the wind tunnel conditions do not map to real-world physics (problem of induction in test design), the analogy fails.","If acting on the bridge design requires an estimate of failure probability which the test cannot provide, the non-probabilistic stance is practically inert."],"shared_structure":["Selection based on survival of stress tests rather than prediction of future performance.","Distinction between operational utility and ontological truth.","Rejection of probabilistic weighting in favor of binary elimination."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_012"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we trust a bridge design that survived wind tunnel tests not because it 'probably' won't collapse, but because it hasn't been shown to fail, we prefer corroborated theories.","The rationality lies in the rigor of the attempt to destroy the candidate, not in the candidate's inherent likelihood of truth."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive 'critical preference' grounded in the logic of testing rather than probability, but accepting this costs the agent any rational assurance that the preferred theory will succeed in future applications.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a positive reason to believe the theory will work (beyond 'not yet failed'), the Popperian defense collapses into induction.","If the definition of 'severe test' implicitly assumes uniformity of nature, the circularity returns.","If practical decision-making inherently demands probabilistic assessment, Popper's binary (falsified/not-falsified) framework is insufficient for action."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'justification of belief' (which requires induction) and 'justification of action' (which relies on the current status of error elimination). By treating corroboration as a report of past severe testing rather than a predictor of future truth, the scientist acts on the theory that has best withstood attempts at falsification, not because it is likely true, but because it is the only one not yet known to be false. This shifts the rational basis from 'expectation of success' to 'avoidance of known error'.","optional_refs":["350739eca4f2195819929272d863a4baaf929266c0b2dcd1506dd8befc5db23e","301c141431fb5df3cd7346d17a2b4a9b2d4b947e5fee6ce5b3b22ef95bef30cd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["350739eca4f2195819929272d863a4baaf929266c0b2dcd1506dd8befc5db23e","301c141431fb5df3cd7346d17a2b4a9b2d4b947e5fee6ce5b3b22ef95bef30cd"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}